@page "/emesis"
@inherits PatientPage

<PageTitle>Emetics and charcoal - Vet Calculator</PageTitle>

<PageHeader Title="Emetics and charcoal" Intro="Doses for inducing emesis in dogs and cats, and for a single dose of activated charcoal." />

<div class="calc-form">
    <SpeciesField />
    <PatientWeightField />
</div>

@if (WeightKg is { } catKg && Species == Species.Cat)
{
    var imMin = catKg * Emetics.CatDexmedetomidineImMinMicrogramsPerKg;
    var imMax = catKg * Emetics.CatDexmedetomidineImMaxMicrogramsPerKg;
    var iv = catKg * Emetics.CatDexmedetomidineIvMicrogramsPerKg;

    <p class="hint">Apomorphine and ropinirole are licensed for dogs only and unreliable in cats, so cats are given an alpha-2 agonist.</p>

    <section class="result-card">
        <h2>Dexmedetomidine, intramuscular</h2>
        <p class="big" id="dexmedetomidine-im">@F(imMin)-@F(imMax) µg = @F(imMin / Emetics.DexmedetomidineMicrogramsPerMl)-@F(imMax / Emetics.DexmedetomidineMicrogramsPerMl) mL</p>
        <p class="hint">7-40 µg/kg IM (Merck Veterinary Manual), of a 0.5 mg/mL solution (Dexdomitor; check the vial).</p>
    </section>

    <section class="result-card">
        <h2>Dexmedetomidine, intravenous</h2>
        <p class="big" id="dexmedetomidine-iv">@F(iv) µg = @F(iv / Emetics.DexmedetomidineMicrogramsPerMl) mL</p>
        <p class="hint">3.5 µg/kg IV (Merck Veterinary Manual), of a 0.5 mg/mL solution.</p>
    </section>
}
else if (WeightKg is { } dogKg)
{
    var kg = dogKg;
    var sc = kg * Emetics.ApomorphineSubcutaneousMgPerKg;
    var ivMin = kg * Emetics.ApomorphineIntravenousMinMgPerKg;
    var ivMax = kg * Emetics.ApomorphineIntravenousMaxMgPerKg;
    var clevor = Emetics.Ropinirole(kg);

    <section class="result-card">
        <h2>Apomorphine, subcutaneous</h2>
        <p class="big" id="apomorphine-sc">@F(sc) mg = @F(sc / Emetics.ApomorphineConcentrationMgPerMl) mL</p>
        <p class="hint">0.1 mg/kg SC of a 1 mg/mL solution (Emedog, Apometic), per the UK data sheet.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="result-card">
        <h2>Apomorphine, intravenous</h2>
        <p class="big">@F(ivMin)-@F(ivMax) mg = @F(ivMin)-@F(ivMax) mL</p>
        <p class="hint">0.03-0.04 mg/kg IV (Merck Veterinary Manual), of a 1 mg/mL solution. Faster than SC; IV use is off-label.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="result-card">
        <h2>Ropinirole eye drops (Clevor)</h2>
        @if (clevor is not null)
        {
            <p class="big" id="clevor">@clevor.Drops @(clevor.Drops == 1 ? "drop" : "drops")</p>
            <p>@clevor.Instructions</p>
            <p class="hint">
                @F(clevor.Micrograms) µg ropinirole, from the data sheet's weight table (target 3.75 mg/m²). If the dog hasn't vomited
                within 15 minutes, the same dose can be repeated 15-20 minutes after the first.
            </p>
        }
        else
        {
            <p>No dose: Clevor's data sheet covers dogs from @F(Emetics.ClevorMinWeightKg) to @F(Emetics.ClevorMaxWeightKg) kg.</p>
        }
    </section>

}

@if (WeightKg is { } weight)
{
    <section class="result-card">
        <h2>Activated charcoal</h2>
        <p class="big" id="charcoal">@F(weight * Emetics.CharcoalMinGramsPerKg)-@F(weight * Emetics.CharcoalMaxGramsPerKg) g</p>
        <p class="hint">
            1-2 g/kg PO, once (Merck Veterinary Manual). For chocolate, Merck advises charcoal only for potentially lethal
            exposures, though Gwaltney-Brant 2001 notes repeated doses usually help symptomatic animals; for cholecalciferol bait it is part of routine decontamination; xylitol is not bound by charcoal. For the volume, divide by the product's strength:
            a 200 mg/mL (20%) suspension would be @F(weight * Emetics.CharcoalMinGramsPerKg * 5)-@F(weight * Emetics.CharcoalMaxGramsPerKg * 5) mL.
            Don't give to a patient that can't protect its airway.
        </p>
    </section>

    <p class="hint">
        How long emesis is worthwhile depends on the toxin. For chocolate, a common rule of thumb is up to about 6 hours
        in a stable patient, because large amounts can form a mass in the stomach (Gwaltney-Brant 2001); no reference
        gives a fixed cut-off, and Merck's "within 2 hours" is an example of a recent exposure, not a limit. For
        anticoagulant and cholecalciferol baits, up to 4 hours (Merck). Only induce emesis in a patient that is clinically normal.
    </p>
    <p class="hint" id="emesis-contraindications">
        Don't induce emesis in a patient that is seizing, sedated or has CNS depression, is hypoxic or dyspnoeic, weak,
        ataxic or comatose, in shock or circulatory failure, or has a reduced gag reflex; or after ingestion of corrosives,
        sharp objects, volatile substances or organic solvents (such as hydrocarbons), or foaming products. Don't give
        apomorphine after a dopamine antagonist (neuroleptic) such as acepromazine. (Emedog and Clevor data sheets.)
    </p>
}
else
{
    <AwaitingInput>Enter the weight to see the doses.</AwaitingInput>
}
